window.PREP_DATA = window.PREP_DATA || {};
window.PREP_DATA.typescript = {
  notes: [
    {
      "title": "What TS adds",
      "body": "TypeScript is JavaScript plus a type system. A type is a label that describes what kind of data a name holds. The compiler checks those labels before the code runs. Then the labels are erased. The browser still runs plain JavaScript. Types help editors, compilers, and humans catch mistakes early."
    },
    {
      "title": "Type vs interface",
      "body": "Both type and interface can describe an object’s shape, meaning which fields it has. An object shape is just that list of fields and their types. Interfaces can merge if you write the same name twice. Types can alias unions, intersections, and mapped types more naturally. A union is this or that. An intersection is both at once. Teams pick one style for objects and stay consistent."
    },
    {
      "title": "Structural typing",
      "body": "TypeScript cares about shape, not class name. If a value has the required fields, it is allowed. Extra properties are allowed when you assign a variable that is already typed. Fresh object literals get an extra check for unknown keys. That extra check catches typos such as nmae."
    },
    {
      "title": "Narrowing",
      "body": "Narrowing means a union gets smaller inside an if. typeof, instanceof, in, and equality all help the checker. A type guard is a test that teaches TypeScript the remaining type. Prefer a shared tag field on each object in a union. That pattern is a discriminated union."
    },
    {
      "title": "Generics",
      "body": "A generic is a placeholder type, often written T. Functions and types can take T so one definition works for many types. Infer T from arguments when you can. T extends Shape limits T so you can use certain fields. Defaults help public APIs when the caller omits T."
    },
    {
      "title": "any vs unknown",
      "body": "any turns the checker off. unknown means you do not know the type yet. You must narrow unknown before you use it. You cannot call methods on unknown until that check. Prefer unknown for untrusted input such as JSON. never means this cannot happen: a throw, or a branch that should be impossible."
    },
    {
      "title": "Utility types",
      "body": "Utility types are built-in helpers that build a new type from an old one. Partial makes every field optional. Required makes every field required. Pick keeps some keys. Omit drops some keys. Record maps keys to a value type. Readonly, ReturnType, and Parameters are other common helpers. Read them in the docs when you need them."
    },
    {
      "title": "null vs undefined",
      "body": "Turn on strictNullChecks so null and undefined are not assignable to every type. Use T | null when empty is on purpose. Optional props are T | undefined. ?? picks a default only for null or undefined. Optional chaining ?. stops if the left side is missing."
    },
    {
      "title": "Modules",
      "body": "Use ES modules: import and export. export type and import type mark type-only exports so they disappear at runtime. skipLibCheck skips checking .d.ts files from libraries and can speed builds. It does not hide errors in your own files. Keep your source strict."
    },
    {
      "title": "tsconfig",
      "body": "tsconfig.json is the TypeScript config file. strict is the baseline bundle of safer checks. target and module control the JavaScript you emit. jsx is for React. paths maps aliases such as @app. noUncheckedIndexedAccess makes dict[key] include undefined. Read the flag you are changing before you turn it on."
    },
    {
      "title": "Declaration files",
      "body": "A .d.ts file describes types only. It does not emit JavaScript from that file. Libraries ship them so you get autocomplete. @types packages on DefinitelyTyped cover many untyped libraries. declare module is a last resort for a package with no types. Prefer real types when they exist."
    },
    {
      "title": "Model the data",
      "body": "Describe app state with unions, not a pile of booleans. Type the public function, the hook, and the API response. Avoid any on those borders. Inside a function, let inference work. Check empty and error cases in the type, not only in comments."
    }
  ],
  questions: [
    { id: 1, level: "beginner", q: "What is TypeScript?", a: "TypeScript is JavaScript plus labels that describe what kind of data you have. Those labels are checked before the code runs. Then they are erased.\n\nThe browser still runs plain JavaScript. Typos and wrong fields get caught in the editor.\n\nIn the code: name must be text. age must be a number. greet takes who as string and returns string. \"Hi \" + who is the body.\n\nA common mistake is expecting types to exist at runtime. They are gone after compile.", code: "const name: string = \"Ada\"; // name must be text\nconst age: number = 21;     // age must be a number\nfunction greet(who: string): string { // a reusable step\n  return \"Hi \" + who;       // return type is text\n}" },
    { id: 2, level: "beginner", q: "Why use TypeScript?", a: "You add TypeScript to catch mistakes before users do. Autocomplete gets smarter. Renames are safer. Types also document what a function expects.\n\nThe cost is a compile step and learning the type system.\n\nIn the code: add requires two numbers and returns a number. add(2, 3) is ok. add(2, \"3\") is commented because the editor errors. Text is not a number.\n\nUse TypeScript on any project where functions are reused and data shapes grow.", code: "function add(a: number, b: number): number { // a reusable step\n  return a + b; // send this back\n}\nadd(2, 3);        // ok\n// add(2, \"3\");   // editor error: text is not a number" },
    { id: 3, level: "beginner", q: "type vs interface?", a: "Both type and interface can describe an object’s shape. interface can be extended and can merge if you write it twice. type can alias unions, primitives, and mapped types more easily.\n\nFor objects they overlap. Pick a team style and stay consistent.\n\nIn the code: User is an interface with name. Id is a type union of string or number. u is a User. id is 7, which fits Id.\n\nUse type for unions. Use either for plain objects.", code: "interface User { // describe the shape\n  name: string; // shows the idea\n}\ntype Id = string | number; // union: type is great here\nconst u: User = { name: \"Ada\" }; // a named box\nconst id: Id = 7; // a named box" },
    { id: 4, level: "beginner", q: "What is a union type?", a: "A | B means the value can be A or B. That is a union. You must narrow (check) before using a field that only one side has.\n\nSuccess | Error is a common result type. You handle both happy and sad paths.\n\nIn the code: Result is ok true with data, or ok false with error. show checks r.ok. After the if, r.data or r.error is allowed. The tag ok is what narrows.\n\nDo not read r.data without checking r.ok. The checker will stop you.", code: "type Result = { ok: true; data: string } | { ok: false; error: string }; // describe the shape\nfunction show(r: Result) { // a reusable step\n  if (r.ok) return r.data;  // narrowed to success\n  return r.error;           // narrowed to error\n}" },
    { id: 5, level: "beginner", q: "What is an intersection type?", a: "A & B must satisfy both shapes at once. That is an intersection. It is useful to combine object types.\n\nIf two sides fight (string & number), you get never, meaning impossible.\n\nIn the code: Named has name. Aged has age. Person is both. p has name Ada and age 21. Both fields are required.\n\nUse & when one object must include every field from two types.", code: "type Named = { name: string }; // describe the shape\ntype Aged = { age: number }; // describe the shape\ntype Person = Named & Aged; // must have both\nconst p: Person = { name: \"Ada\", age: 21 }; // a named box" },
    { id: 6, level: "beginner", q: "What does optional ? mean?", a: "A property with ? may be missing. A parameter with ? can be skipped. The type is often T | undefined.\n\nForms and configs that are not always filled use this.\n\nIn the code: User has name and optional nickname. greet has optional title. If title is present, it prefixes name. u skips nickname.\n\nDo not treat optional as null. It is usually undefined when missing.", code: "type User = { name: string; nickname?: string }; // describe the shape\nfunction greet(name: string, title?: string) { // a reusable step\n  return title ? title + \" \" + name : name; // send this back\n}\nconst u: User = { name: \"Ada\" }; // nickname skipped" },
    { id: 7, level: "beginner", q: "any vs unknown vs never?", a: "any turns the checker off. unknown is “I do not know yet”: you must check before using. never is “this cannot happen”: throws, infinite loops, or impossible branches.\n\nPrefer unknown for untrusted input.\n\nIn the code: handle takes unknown. After typeof x === \"string\", toUpperCase is safe. fail’s return type is never because it always throws.\n\nDo not type JSON as any. Start with unknown and narrow.", code: "function handle(x: unknown) { // a reusable step\n  if (typeof x === \"string\") { // pick a path\n    return x.toUpperCase(); // safe after check\n  }\n}\nfunction fail(msg: string): never { // a reusable step\n  throw new Error(msg);     // never returns\n}" },
    { id: 8, level: "beginner", q: "void vs undefined?", a: "void means ignore whatever comes back. undefined is the actual value undefined. Callbacks are often typed void so they may still return something unused.\n\nPrefer void when there is no useful result.\n\nIn the code: log returns void and only console.logs. done is typed undefined and set to undefined. log(\"hi\") runs the function.\n\nUse void on event handlers. Use undefined when the value undefined is part of your data.", code: "function log(msg: string): void { // a reusable step\n  console.log(msg);         // no useful return\n}\nconst done: undefined = undefined; // a named box\nlog(\"hi\"); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 9, level: "beginner", q: "How do you type an array?", a: "string[] or Array<string> means a list of strings. A tuple is a short list with a known type per slot: [string, number]. ReadonlyArray<T> says do not mutate, in the type system.\n\nYou then cannot push a number into a name list.\n\nIn the code: names is string[]. pair is a tuple [string, number]. frozen is ReadonlyArray<number>. names.push(\"Cyd\") is allowed because names is a normal array.\n\nUse a tuple when index 0 and index 1 mean different things.", code: "const names: string[] = [\"Ada\", \"Bob\"]; // a named box\nconst pair: [string, number] = [\"Ada\", 21]; // tuple\nconst frozen: ReadonlyArray<number> = [1, 2]; // a named box\nnames.push(\"Cyd\"); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 10, level: "beginner", q: "How do you type a function?", a: "Write types on parameters and the return. You can also write a function type: (x: number) => string. Use overloads only when one signature is not enough.\n\nPublic APIs need these labels.\n\nIn the code: double takes n: number and returns number. fn is typed (x: number) => string and uses String(x). The log prints double(3) and fn(3).\n\nIf you skip the return type, inference often still works. Annotate public functions anyway.", code: "function double(n: number): number { // a reusable step\n  return n * 2; // send this back\n}\nconst fn: (x: number) => string = (x) => String(x); // a named box\nconsole.log(double(3), fn(3)); // print to see it" },
    { id: 11, level: "beginner", q: "What is type inference?", a: "Inference means TypeScript guesses the type from the value and from return statements. Annotate public functions and tricky spots. Let local variables infer.\n\nFiles stay readable when you do not label every line.\n\nIn the code: n is 21, inferred as number. hi takes string and returns a string by inference. msg is the result of hi(\"Ada\").\n\nIf inference becomes unknown or any, add an annotation.", code: "const n = 21;              // inferred as number\nfunction hi(name: string) { // a reusable step\n  return \"Hi \" + name;     // inferred return: string\n}\nconst msg = hi(\"Ada\"); // a named box" },
    { id: 12, level: "beginner", q: "What is a type assertion?", a: "as Type tells the compiler to treat a value as that type. It is not a runtime conversion. Nothing is checked at run time.\n\nDouble assertion via unknown is a smell. Prefer narrowing.\n\nIn the code: querySelector is asserted as HTMLElement. textContent = \"Hi\" is then allowed. The comment says still check null in real apps. The assertion does not prove the node exists.\n\nIf the node might be missing, check for null instead of asserting.", code: "// Example: What is a type assertion?\nconst el = document.querySelector(\"#app\") as HTMLElement; // a named box\nel.textContent = \"Hi\";     // we promised it is an element\n// still check null in real apps when it might be missing" },
    { id: 13, level: "beginner", q: "What is as const?", a: "as const keeps the exact literal types and makes fields readonly. \"click\" stays \"click\", not general string. Useful for action names and config objects.\n\nUnions of literals stay tight.\n\nIn the code: modes is [\"light\", \"dark\"] as const. Mode is typeof modes[number], so \"light\" | \"dark\". action.type is \"inc\", not string.\n\nUse as const on lists of allowed strings so the union does not widen.", code: "const modes = [\"light\", \"dark\"] as const; // a named box\ntype Mode = typeof modes[number]; // \"light\" | \"dark\"\nconst action = { type: \"inc\" } as const; // a named box\n// action.type is \"inc\", not string" },
    { id: 14, level: "beginner", q: "enum vs union of literals?", a: "A union 'a' | 'b' erases at compile time and is often preferred. enum compiles to a JavaScript object unless const enum. Numeric enums have reverse-mapping quirks.\n\nChoose a style and stay consistent.\n\nIn the code: Color is \"red\" | \"blue\". c is \"red\". Dir is an enum with Up and Down. d is Dir.Up. The union has no runtime object.\n\nPrefer string unions unless you need a runtime enum object.", code: "type Color = \"red\" | \"blue\"; // simple union\nconst c: Color = \"red\"; // a named box\nenum Dir { Up, Down }        // runtime object\nconst d: Dir = Dir.Up; // a named box" },
    { id: 15, level: "beginner", q: "What is a tuple?", a: "A tuple has a known length and a type per index. Example: [number, string]. You can have optional and rest slots too.\n\nPairs like [x, y] or CSV columns fit this.\n\nIn the code: Pair is [number, string]. p is [21, \"Ada\"]. age is p[0], a number. name is p[1], a string.\n\nDo not use a tuple for a list that can grow. Use T[] for that.", code: "type Pair = [number, string]; // describe the shape\nconst p: Pair = [21, \"Ada\"]; // a named box\nconst age = p[0];           // number\nconst name = p[1];          // string" },
    { id: 16, level: "intermediate", q: "What is structural typing?", a: "If a value has the required fields, it is allowed, even if it was not declared as that type. That is structural typing. Java is more “same class name.” Private class fields are a special case.\n\nExtra fields on a variable can still be assigned to a smaller type.\n\nIn the code: Named needs name. user has name and age. n: Named = user is allowed because the shape fits. age is extra.\n\nDo not expect two types with the same fields to fail just because they have different names.", code: "// Example: What is structural typing?\ntype Named = { name: string }; // describe the shape\nconst user = { name: \"Ada\", age: 21 }; // a named box\nconst n: Named = user;      // extra age is ok (shape fits)" },
    { id: 17, level: "intermediate", q: "excess property checks?", a: "When you assign a fresh { } to a typed variable, unknown keys are flagged. That catches typos like nmae. Once the object lives in a wider variable, extra keys are allowed.\n\nThat is the excess property check.\n\nIn the code: ok has name and age. u: User = ok allows extra age via the variable. The bad literal with nmae is commented because the typo is caught.\n\nIf a typo is not caught, you probably assigned through a wider variable.", code: "type User = { name: string }; // describe the shape\nconst ok = { name: \"Ada\", age: 21 }; // a named box\nconst u: User = ok;         // extra age allowed via variable\n// const bad: User = { name: \"Ada\", nmae: \"x\" }; // typo caught" },
    { id: 18, level: "intermediate", q: "What is narrowing?", a: "Narrowing means TypeScript follows your checks so a union gets smaller. typeof, ===, in, instanceof, and Array.isArray all help. Be careful: 0 and \"\" are falsy.\n\nThen you can use the right fields safely.\n\nIn the code: len takes string | string[]. typeof x === \"string\" narrows to string. The other branch is the array. Both use .length.\n\nUse typeof for primitives. Do not use if (x) when 0 is valid.", code: "function len(x: string | string[]) { // a reusable step\n  if (typeof x === \"string\") { // pick a path\n    return x.length;        // string\n  }\n  return x.length;          // array\n}" },
    { id: 19, level: "intermediate", q: "discriminated union?", a: "A discriminated union is a union with a shared tag field, such as type: \"ok\" or type: \"err\". switch on that tag and TypeScript knows the rest of the shape.\n\nThis is the best way to model app state. A pile of booleans is weaker.\n\nIn the code: State is type ok with data, or type err with error. msg checks s.type === \"ok\" then returns s.data or s.error. The tag type is the discriminator.\n\nGive every variant a unique literal tag.", code: "type State = // describe the shape\n  | { type: \"ok\"; data: string }\n  | { type: \"err\"; error: string }; // one CSS rule\nfunction msg(s: State) { // a reusable step\n  if (s.type === \"ok\") return s.data; // send this back\n  return s.error; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 20, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a type guard?", a: "A type guard is a test that narrows a union. User-defined: function isFish(p): p is Fish. Prefer in or a discriminator when you can.\n\nCustom objects from APIs often need a guard.\n\nIn the code: Fish has swim. Bird has fly. isFish returns p is Fish by checking \"swim\" in p. After if (isFish(p)), p.swim is allowed.\n\nThe p is Fish return type is what teaches the checker. A plain boolean is not enough.", code: "type Fish = { swim: () => void }; // describe the shape\ntype Bird = { fly: () => void }; // describe the shape\nfunction isFish(p: Fish | Bird): p is Fish { // a reusable step\n  return \"swim\" in p;       // type guard\n}" },
    { id: 21, level: "intermediate", q: "assertion function?", a: "An assertion function uses asserts in the return type. After assert(cond) the checker treats cond as true. If the condition fails, you throw.\n\nThis is handy at function borders so you do not repeat the same if.\n\nIn the code: assert throws if !cond. start takes string | null, calls assert(x, \"need text\"), then x.toUpperCase(). After assert, x is string.\n\nUse this when a missing value should crash with a clear message.", code: "function assert(cond: unknown, msg: string): asserts cond { // a reusable step\n  if (!cond) throw new Error(msg); // pick a path\n}\nfunction start(x: string | null) { // a reusable step\n  assert(x, \"need text\"); // shows the idea\n  return x.toUpperCase();   // x is string here\n}" },
    { id: 22, level: "intermediate", q: "What are generics?", a: "A generic is a placeholder type. function identity<T>(x: T): T relates input to output without any. TypeScript often infers T from the argument.\n\nReusable lists and functions use this.\n\nIn the code: identity returns x as T. identity(21) infers number. identity(\"Ada\") infers string. T is different on each call.\n\nIf you used any, the return type would not follow the input.", code: "function identity<T>(x: T): T { // a reusable step\n  return x;                 // same type in and out\n}\nconst n = identity(21);     // T is number\nconst s = identity(\"Ada\");  // T is string" },
    { id: 23, level: "intermediate", q: "generic constraints?", a: "T extends Something limits T so you can use certain fields. T extends { id: string } lets you read t.id. Keep the constraint as small as the function needs.\n\nThen generics stay useful, not empty.\n\nIn the code: idOf requires T to have id: string. It returns item.id. The call passes { id: \"u1\", name: \"Ada\" }. name is extra and fine.\n\nDo not constrain T to a huge type if you only need id.", code: "function idOf<T extends { id: string }>(item: T): string { // a reusable step\n  return item.id;           // allowed because of extends\n}\nidOf({ id: \"u1\", name: \"Ada\" }); // one CSS rule" },
    { id: 24, level: "intermediate", q: "What is keyof?", a: "keyof User is the union of property names, like \"id\" | \"email\". You use it with T[K] to get the value type.\n\nTyped picks and helpers use keyof.\n\nIn the code: User has id and email. Keys is keyof User. get takes K extends Keys and returns u[k]. The key and the value stay linked.\n\nIf K is not keyof User, u[k] would be unsafe.", code: "type User = { id: string; email: string }; // describe the shape\ntype Keys = keyof User;     // \"id\" | \"email\"\nfunction get<K extends Keys>(u: User, k: K) { // a reusable step\n  return u[k]; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 25, level: "intermediate", q: "indexed access types?", a: "User[\"email\"] is the type of that property. That is indexed access. User[keyof User] is a union of all value types.\n\nYou want “whatever this field is” without copying.\n\nIn the code: Age is User[\"age\"], which is number. Values is User[keyof User], string | number. User has id and age.\n\nUse T[K] in generic helpers instead of repeating the field type.", code: "// Example: indexed access types?\ntype User = { id: string; age: number }; // describe the shape\ntype Age = User[\"age\"];     // number\ntype Values = User[keyof User]; // string | number" },
    { id: 26, level: "intermediate", q: "typeof as a type operator?", a: "typeof in type position copies the type from a value: type Cfg = typeof config. Combine with keyof for maps of keys to handlers.\n\nRuntime typeof is different: it returns a string.\n\nIn the code: config has port and name. Cfg is typeof config. copy must match { port: number; name: string }. The value is the source of truth.\n\nIf you change config, Cfg follows. You do not update two copies.", code: "// Example: typeof as a type operator?\nconst config = { port: 3000, name: \"app\" }; // a named box\ntype Cfg = typeof config;   // { port: number; name: string }\nconst copy: Cfg = { port: 80, name: \"x\" }; // a named box" },
    { id: 27, level: "intermediate", q: "typeof vs instanceof at runtime?", a: "typeof works for primitives: string, number, boolean, function. instanceof works for class instances. TypeScript narrows using both.\n\nPick the right test.\n\nIn the code: dump takes Date | string. instanceof Date uses toISOString. typeof x === \"string\" returns x. Each branch is narrowed.\n\nDo not use typeof on a Date. typeof date is \"object\". Use instanceof.", code: "function dump(x: Date | string) { // a reusable step\n  if (x instanceof Date) return x.toISOString(); // send this back\n  if (typeof x === \"string\") return x; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 28, level: "intermediate", q: "Partial and Required?", a: "Partial<T> makes every field optional. Required<T> makes every field required. They walk keyof T.\n\nUse Partial for patch updates. Use Required for complete records.\n\nIn the code: User has name and age. Patch is Partial<User>, so p can be { age: 22 }. Full is Required<Patch>, putting both fields back.\n\nA PATCH endpoint often takes Partial<User>, not User.", code: "type User = { name: string; age: number }; // describe the shape\ntype Patch = Partial<User>; // name? age?\nconst p: Patch = { age: 22 }; // a named box\ntype Full = Required<Patch>; // describe the shape" },
    { id: 29, level: "intermediate", q: "Pick and Omit?", a: "Pick<T, \"a\" | \"b\"> keeps those keys. Omit<T, \"a\"> removes them. Great for variants of a data object.\n\nThe UI often needs fewer fields than the database.\n\nIn the code: Public is Omit<User, \"secret\">. Ids is Pick<User, \"id\" | \"name\">. p has id and name, no secret. User also had secret.\n\nOmit secrets before you pass a user to the client.", code: "type User = { id: string; name: string; secret: string }; // describe the shape\ntype Public = Omit<User, \"secret\">; // describe the shape\ntype Ids = Pick<User, \"id\" | \"name\">; // describe the shape\nconst p: Public = { id: \"1\", name: \"Ada\" }; // a named box" },
    { id: 30, level: "intermediate", q: "Record?", a: "Record<K, V> is an object whose keys are K and values are V. Record<string, number> is a string-to-number map.\n\nUseful for dictionaries and lookup tables.\n\nIn the code: ages maps Ada and Bob to numbers. n is ages[\"Ada\"]. The object is typed as Record<string, number>.\n\nWithout Record, a random string key might not type-check the same way. With noUncheckedIndexedAccess, ages[\"Ada\"] can also be undefined.", code: "const ages: Record<string, number> = { // a named box\n  Ada: 21,\n  Bob: 22\n};\nconst n = ages[\"Ada\"]; // a named box" },
    { id: 31, level: "intermediate", q: "ReturnType and Parameters?", a: "ReturnType<typeof fn> is what fn returns. Parameters<typeof fn> is the tuple of arguments. Useful when wrapping APIs.\n\nYou do not copy types by hand.\n\nIn the code: makeUser returns { name, age }. U is that return type. Args is [string, number]. The function is the source of both types.\n\nIf you change makeUser, U and Args follow.", code: "function makeUser(name: string, age: number) { // a reusable step\n  return { name, age }; // send this back\n}\ntype U = ReturnType<typeof makeUser>; // describe the shape\ntype Args = Parameters<typeof makeUser>; // [string, number]" },
    { id: 32, level: "intermediate", q: "Awaited?", a: "Awaited<Promise<User>> is User. It unwraps nested promises too. Handy with ReturnType of async functions.\n\nYou type the data, not the ticket.\n\nIn the code: load returns Promise<{ name: string }>. User is Awaited<ReturnType<typeof load>>, so { name: string }. You get the resolved shape.\n\nDo not type the variable as Promise when you already awaited.", code: "async function load(): Promise<{ name: string }> { // a future result\n  return { name: \"Ada\" }; // send this back\n}\ntype User = Awaited<ReturnType<typeof load>>; // { name: string }" },
    { id: 33, level: "intermediate", q: "NonNullable, Extract, Exclude?", a: "NonNullable removes null and undefined. Exclude<T, U> drops members assignable to U. Extract keeps members assignable to U.\n\nYou filter unions with these.\n\nIn the code: T is string | number | null. A is NonNullable<T>, string | number. B is Exclude<A, number>, string. C is Extract<A, number>, number.\n\nUse Exclude when you want “the union without this member.”", code: "type T = string | number | null; // describe the shape\ntype A = NonNullable<T>;        // string | number\ntype B = Exclude<A, number>;    // string\ntype C = Extract<A, number>;    // number" },
    { id: 34, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a mapped type?", a: "A mapped type transforms every property of T: { [K in keyof T]: ... }. You can add | null, or flip optional with -?. Partial and Readonly are mapped types.\n\nYou generate variants of a shape.\n\nIn the code: Nullable<T> maps each key to T[K] | null. User has name: string. MaybeUser is { name: string | null }. Every field becomes nullable.\n\nWrite a mapped type when you would otherwise copy the shape by hand.", code: "// Example: What is a mapped type?\ntype Nullable<T> = { [K in keyof T]: T[K] | null }; // describe the shape\ntype User = { name: string }; // describe the shape\ntype MaybeUser = Nullable<User>; // { name: string | null }" },
    { id: 35, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a conditional type?", a: "T extends U ? X : Y is an if/else for types. A conditional type. Naked type parameters distribute over unions. Exclude is built this way.\n\nSmart return types use this.\n\nIn the code: IsString<T> is true if T extends string. A is IsString<\"hi\">, true. B is IsString<number>, false.\n\nIf T is a union, the conditional may run per member. That is distribution.", code: "// Example: What is a conditional type?\ntype IsString<T> = T extends string ? true : false; // describe the shape\ntype A = IsString<\"hi\">;    // true\ntype B = IsString<number>;  // false" },
    { id: 36, level: "advanced", q: "distributive conditional types?", a: "If T is a bare type parameter, T extends U ? X : Y runs per union member. Wrap as [T] to disable that. That is how Exclude works.\n\nA type can “explode” in a way you did not want.\n\nIn the code: Box<T> is T[] if T extends any. A is Box<string | number>, which distributes to string[] | number[]. B uses a tuple wrap so it does not distribute the same way.\n\nIf you wanted (string | number)[], disable distribution with a tuple wrap.", code: "// Example: distributive conditional types?\ntype Box<T> = T extends any ? T[] : never; // describe the shape\ntype A = Box<string | number>; // string[] | number[] (distributed)\ntype B = [string | number] extends any ? 1 : 0; // describe the shape" },
    { id: 37, level: "advanced", q: "infer keyword?", a: "Inside a conditional, infer R captures a slot. T extends Promise<infer U> ? U : T unwraps a promise. ReturnType is built on this idea.\n\nYou write utilities that pull a piece out of a type.\n\nIn the code: Unwrap<Promise<string>> is string. Unwrap<number> is number. infer U is the inner type of Promise.\n\nUse infer when you need “whatever is inside this wrapper.”", code: "// Example: infer keyword?\ntype Unwrap<T> = T extends Promise<infer U> ? U : T; // a future result\ntype A = Unwrap<Promise<string>>; // string\ntype B = Unwrap<number>;          // number" },
    { id: 38, level: "advanced", q: "template literal types?", a: "Template literal types join and split string unions with backticks in the type. type EventName = `on${Capitalize<string>}`. Useful for routes, CSS, and events.\n\nTyped APIs that look like strings use this.\n\nIn the code: Color is \"red\" | \"blue\". Handler is \"onRed\" | \"onBlue\". h is \"onRed\". Capitalize turns red into Red.\n\nIf you add \"green\" to Color, Handler gains \"onGreen\" automatically.", code: "// Example: template literal types?\ntype Color = \"red\" | \"blue\"; // describe the shape\ntype Handler = `on${Capitalize<Color>}`; // \"onRed\" | \"onBlue\"\nconst h: Handler = \"onRed\"; // a named box" },
    { id: 39, level: "advanced", q: "What is satisfies?", a: "satisfies checks that a value fits a type without widening the value’s inferred type. You keep literal inference and still validate the shape.\n\nConfig objects use this.\n\nIn the code: cfg satisfies Cfg, so theme must exist. cfg.theme is \"dark\", not string. t is that literal. Cfg only said theme: string.\n\nUse satisfies when as const is too tight and a plain annotation is too wide.", code: "type Cfg = { theme: string }; // describe the shape\nconst cfg = { theme: \"dark\" } satisfies Cfg; // a named box\n// cfg.theme is \"dark\", not string\nconst t = cfg.theme; // a named box" },
    { id: 40, level: "intermediate", q: "const type parameters?", a: "<const T> makes the generic infer T as a readonly literal instead of widening. Helpful for typed routes and tuple configs.\n\n[\"a\",\"b\"] stays those two strings.\n\nIn the code: pair<const T> returns x as T. p is pair([\"a\", \"b\"] as const), so readonly [\"a\", \"b\"]. Without const, it might become string[].\n\nUse const type parameters when the exact literals matter.", code: "function pair<const T>(x: T): T { // a reusable step\n  return x; // send this back\n}\nconst p = pair([\"a\", \"b\"] as const); // a named box\n// p is readonly [\"a\", \"b\"]" },
    { id: 41, level: "intermediate", q: "strictNullChecks?", a: "strictNullChecks means null and undefined are not assignable to every type. You must handle them with checks or unions.\n\nThis flag alone prevents a huge class of crashes. It matters more than fancy generics.\n\nIn the code: len(s: string) cannot take null. len(null) is commented as an error. len2 takes string | null and uses s?.length ?? 0.\n\nTurn this flag on before you add complex utility types.", code: "function len(s: string) { // a reusable step\n  return s.length; // send this back\n}\n// len(null); // error with strictNullChecks\nfunction len2(s: string | null) { // a reusable step\n  return s?.length ?? 0; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 42, level: "intermediate", q: "noUncheckedIndexedAccess?", a: "noUncheckedIndexedAccess makes dict[key] and arr[i] include undefined. Dictionaries and arrays might not have that slot. The flag makes the type honest.\n\nSlightly more verbose, safer.\n\nIn the code: ages[\"Bob\"] is number | undefined with the flag. safe is n ?? 0. Bob is missing from the object.\n\nHandle undefined after every index read when this flag is on.", code: "// Example: noUncheckedIndexedAccess?\nconst ages: Record<string, number> = { Ada: 21 }; // a named box\nconst n = ages[\"Bob\"];      // number | undefined (with the flag)\nconst safe = n ?? 0; // a named box" },
    { id: 43, level: "intermediate", q: "strict function types?", a: "Under strictFunctionTypes, function parameters are checked more carefully (contravariant). That stops unsafe callback assignment. Methods are historically looser.\n\nPassing handlers around needs this.\n\nIn the code: Handler takes string | number. h logs x. h(\"a\") is valid. The parameter type matches the alias.\n\nIf you tried to assign (x: string) => void to Handler, strict function types can reject it, because Handler may pass a number.", code: "type Handler = (x: string | number) => void; // describe the shape\nconst h: Handler = (x: string | number) => { // a named box\n  console.log(x); // print to see it\n};\nh(\"a\"); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 44, level: "advanced", q: "covariance vs contravariance?", a: "Returns can be more specific (covariant): a Dog function can be used as Animal return. Parameters should be more general (contravariant) for safety.\n\nTS methods are bivariant for old reasons; function types are stricter. This shows up when you assign callbacks.\n\nIn the code: getDog returns Dog. getAnimal: () => Animal = getDog is allowed because return is covariant. Dog is a more specific Animal.\n\nDo not assign a function that only accepts Dog where a function must accept any Animal.", code: "type Animal = { kind: string }; // describe the shape\ntype Dog = Animal & { bark: true }; // describe the shape\nconst getDog = (): Dog => ({ kind: \"dog\", bark: true }); // a named box\nconst getAnimal: () => Animal = getDog; // return is covariant" },
    { id: 45, level: "intermediate", q: "What is declaration merging?", a: "Interfaces with the same name merge their fields. type aliases do not merge. Also used for module augmentation.\n\nYou extend library types this way.\n\nIn the code: two interface User blocks add name and age. u needs both. They merged into one User. That is declaration merging.\n\nIf you meant two different shapes, use two names. Accidental merge is a bug.", code: "interface User { // describe the shape\n  name: string; // shows the idea\n}\ninterface User { // describe the shape\n  age: number;              // merged\n}\nconst u: User = { name: \"Ada\", age: 21 }; // a named box" },
    { id: 46, level: "intermediate", q: "module augmentation?", a: "Module augmentation adds fields to a library’s types: declare module \"lib\" { interface Foo { extra: boolean } }. Keep it in a .d.ts included by the project.\n\nPlugins use this.\n\nIn the code: express-session’s SessionData gets userId: string. That extra field is what we added. SessionData is the library interface.\n\nAugment the real interface. Do not copy the whole library types by hand.", code: "declare module \"express-session\" {\n  interface SessionData { // describe the shape\n    userId: string;         // extra field we added\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 47, level: "intermediate", q: "What are ambient declarations?", a: "Ambient declarations describe a value that exists at runtime elsewhere: a global, a CDN script. declare const API_KEY: string. You write declare without a body.\n\nTypeScript then knows about things not written in this file.\n\nIn the code: APP_NAME is declared as string. title() returns APP_NAME.toUpperCase(). A script tag provides the value at runtime.\n\nIf the script is missing, the type will not save you. The name is still undefined at runtime.", code: "declare const APP_NAME: string; // provided by a script tag\nfunction title() { // a reusable step\n  return APP_NAME.toUpperCase(); // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 48, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a .d.ts file?", a: "A .d.ts file holds types only. It does not emit JavaScript from that file. Libraries ship them so you get autocomplete. export {} makes a file a module so you do not pollute globals by accident.\n\nYou consume JS packages through these files.\n\nIn the code: the comment shows foo.d.ts exporting add(a, b): number. app.ts imports add and TS knows the types. There is no JS body in the .d.ts.\n\nDo not put runtime code in a .d.ts. It will not run.", code: "// Example: What is a .d.ts file?\n// in foo.d.ts\nexport function add(a: number, b: number): number; // share this name\n// in app.ts you import add and TS knows the types" },
    { id: 49, level: "beginner", q: "How do you type React props?", a: "Make a Props type with each field. Destructure in the function. children?: React.ReactNode. Then you cannot forget a required prop.\n\nThis is the everyday React plus TypeScript pattern.\n\nIn the code: Props has title and onClose. Modal takes Props. The button shows title and calls onClose. Both fields are required.\n\nExport Props if other files need the same shape.", code: "type Props = { title: string; onClose: () => void }; // describe the shape\nfunction Modal({ title, onClose }: Props) { // a reusable step\n  return <button onClick={onClose}>{title}</button>; // when the user acts\n}" },
    { id: 50, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you type useState?", a: "useState(0) infers number. useState<User | null>(null) when the start value is not enough. Data that starts empty needs the generic.\n\nTell the drawer “this will be a user or empty,” not just empty.\n\nIn the code: n infers number from 0. user is User | null starting at null. setUser({ name: \"Ada\" }) is then allowed. Without the generic, null would infer null only.\n\nAlways pass the generic when the initial value is null.", code: "type User = { name: string }; // describe the shape\nconst [n] = useState(0);                 // number\nconst [user, setUser] = useState<User | null>(null); // remember a value\nsetUser({ name: \"Ada\" }); // one CSS rule" },
    { id: 51, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you type events?", a: "ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement> is for inputs. FormEvent<HTMLFormElement> is for submit. MouseEvent<HTMLButtonElement> is for clicks.\n\nThen e.target.value is typed.\n\nIn the code: onChange logs e.target.value as string. onSubmit calls preventDefault. The generics name the element. That is how value is a string.\n\nIf you type the event as any, you lose that safety.", code: "function onChange(e: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) { // when the user acts\n  console.log(e.target.value); // string\n}\nfunction onSubmit(e: React.FormEvent<HTMLFormElement>) { // a reusable step\n  e.preventDefault(); // shows the idea\n}" },
    { id: 52, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you type a generic list component?", a: "A generic list takes items: T[] and render: (item: T) => ReactNode. The caller infers T from items. Tables of any row shape use this.\n\nT is the row type.\n\nIn the code: Props<T> has items and render. List maps with items.map(render). T is inferred from the items you pass. The fragment wraps the result.\n\nIf render’s argument does not match T, the checker errors.", code: "type Props<T> = { items: T[]; render: (item: T) => React.ReactNode }; // describe the shape\nfunction List<T>({ items, render }: Props<T>) { // a reusable step\n  return <>{items.map(render)}</>; // make a new list\n}" },
    { id: 53, level: "beginner", q: "How do you type fetch JSON?", a: "The compiler cannot know what the server sent. unknown plus a type guard or a schema library is safer than as User. as User is a promise you might break at runtime.\n\nAPIs lie.\n\nIn the code: load fetches /api/me and returns res.json() as Promise<unknown>. You must check before you use name. There is no assertion here.\n\nDo not type json() as User without a check.", code: "async function load(): Promise<unknown> { // a future result\n  const res = await fetch(\"/api/me\"); // ask the network\n  return res.json();          // unknown until you check\n}" },
    { id: 54, level: "advanced", q: "Why is `as User` dangerous?", a: "as User silences the checker without validating fields. A missing property becomes a crash later. Prefer a validator that returns User or throws.\n\nProduction APIs need this.\n\nIn the code: parseUser checks data is an object with \"name\" in it, then returns data as User. Otherwise it throws. The assertion is after a check, which is better.\n\nA bare as User on JSON.parse is the dangerous version.", code: "type User = { name: string }; // describe the shape\nfunction parseUser(data: unknown): User { // a reusable step\n  if (data && typeof data === \"object\" && \"name\" in data) { // pick a path\n    return data as User;      // still better after a check\n  }\n  throw new Error(\"bad user\"); // shows the idea\n}" },
    { id: 55, level: "intermediate", q: "zod or similar with TS?", a: "A schema library lets you write one schema. z.infer<typeof schema> gives the type. Runtime checks and compile-time types stay in sync.\n\nYou do not maintain two copies.\n\nIn the code: the comment sketches z.infer. schema has name: \"string\". User is { name: string }. u is { name: \"Ada\" }. A real Zod schema would both parse and infer.\n\nWhen the schema changes, infer the type. Do not copy fields into a second interface.", code: "// idea of z.infer without the library:\nconst schema = { name: \"string\" as const }; // a named box\ntype User = { name: string }; // inferred from a real schema\nconst u: User = { name: \"Ada\" }; // a named box" },
    { id: 56, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a branded type?", a: "A branded type is UserId = string & { readonly brand: unique symbol }. You cannot pass a random string where UserId is required. Runtime is still a string.\n\nIds that look alike stay distinct in the type system.\n\nIn the code: asUserId casts s as UserId. id is asUserId(\"u1\"). The brand field is only in the type. At runtime id is still \"u1\".\n\nUse a helper to create brands. Do not scatter as UserId everywhere.", code: "type UserId = string & { readonly brand: unique symbol }; // describe the shape\nfunction asUserId(s: string): UserId { // a reusable step\n  return s as UserId; // send this back\n}\nconst id = asUserId(\"u1\"); // a named box" },
    { id: 57, level: "intermediate", q: "opaque / branded ids why?", a: "Branding stops passing an OrderId where a UserId is required. Cheap safety for domain models. Big codebases mix ids otherwise.\n\nTwo string ids are not the same kind of id.\n\nIn the code: UserId and OrderId are different brands. findUser takes UserId. u is \"u1\" as UserId. findUser(u) is ok. An OrderId would not fit.\n\nBrand every id type that you might mix up.", code: "type UserId = string & { _u: true }; // describe the shape\ntype OrderId = string & { _o: true }; // describe the shape\nfunction findUser(id: UserId) { // a reusable step\n  return id; // send this back\n}\nconst u = \"u1\" as UserId; // a named box\nfindUser(u); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 58, level: "beginner", q: "What does tsconfig strict include?", a: "strict: true is a bundle: no implicit any, strict null checks, stricter functions, and more. Version by version a few extra flags join the bundle.\n\nThis is the baseline for serious projects. You do not need every sub-flag memorized.\n\nIn the code: add annotates a, b, and the return. add(1, 2) is checked. Strict wants those annotations on parameters.\n\nTurn on strict in tsconfig before you add more flags.", code: "function add(a: number, b: number): number { // a reusable step\n  return a + b;             // strict wants these annotations\n}\nadd(1, 2); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 59, level: "intermediate", q: "noImplicitAny?", a: "noImplicitAny means if TypeScript would guess any, it errors instead. You must annotate parameters. That forces you to think about public inputs.\n\nany then does not spread.\n\nIn the code: greet(name: string) must annotate with the flag. It returns \"Hi \" + name. greet(\"Ada\") is fine. Without the annotation, name would be an error.\n\nIf a parameter is implicit any, add a type.", code: "function greet(name: string) { // must annotate with the flag\n  return \"Hi \" + name; // send this back\n}\ngreet(\"Ada\"); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 60, level: "intermediate", q: "isolatedModules?", a: "isolatedModules means every file must be transpileable alone, without the whole program. Some type-only patterns are banned. Prefer import type and export type.\n\nVite and modern bundlers need this.\n\nIn the code: import type { User } is type-only. export type { User } re-exports the type. hi(u: User) returns u.name. No runtime import of User.\n\nIf you import a type as a value, isolatedModules can fail.", code: "import type { User } from \"./user\"; // type-only import\nexport type { User }; // share this name\nfunction hi(u: User) { // a reusable step\n  return u.name; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 61, level: "intermediate", q: "esModuleInterop?", a: "esModuleInterop makes import React from \"react\" work more often with CommonJS. Most React apps enable it. Understand default vs namespace imports.\n\nIt helps when a package exports module.exports = fn.\n\nIn the code: import fs from \"fs\" is a friendlier default import. read uses fs.readFileSync. utf8 is the encoding.\n\nIf default import fails, try import * as fs, or enable this flag.", code: "import fs from \"fs\";        // friendlier default import\nfunction read(path: string) { // a reusable step\n  return fs.readFileSync(path, \"utf8\"); // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 62, level: "intermediate", q: "moduleResolution bundler vs node?", a: "moduleResolution bundler matches Vite/webpack: extensions optional, package exports. node16/nodenext is stricter about .js extensions and exports maps.\n\nImports that type-check should also run.\n\nIn the code: import { add } from \"./math\" has no extension, which bundler often allows. useAdd calls add(a, b). The re-export is useAdd.\n\nIf tsc is fine but Node crashes, your resolution mode does not match runtime.", code: "import { add } from \"./math\"; // bundler often allows no extension\nexport function useAdd(a: number, b: number) { // share this name\n  return add(a, b); // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 63, level: "beginner", q: "Does TypeScript run in the browser?", a: "TypeScript does not run in the browser. The browser runs the compiled JavaScript. Source maps let you debug the original TS in DevTools.\n\nDo not expect types at runtime.\n\nIn the code: n: number = 21. console.log(n) at runtime is just console.log(21). The : number label is gone. The comment says so.\n\nIf you need a check at runtime, write a function. The type will not do it.", code: "// Example: Does TypeScript run in the browser?\nconst n: number = 21; // a named box\nconsole.log(n);             // runtime is just console.log(21)\n// the : number label is gone at runtime" },
    { id: 64, level: "intermediate", q: "emit vs transpile-only?", a: "tsc can type-check and emit JS. swc/babel often transpile without full checking. CI should still run tsc --noEmit.\n\nSpeed tools then do not hide errors.\n\nIn the code: add(a: number, b: number) is what tsc --noEmit checks. add(1, 2) is a valid call. Bundlers may still emit JS even if types are wrong, unless CI runs tsc.\n\nKeep tsc --noEmit in CI even if Vite compiles.", code: "function add(a: number, b: number) { // a reusable step\n  return a + b;             // tsc --noEmit checks this\n}\nadd(1, 2); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 65, level: "advanced", q: "project references?", a: "Project references split a monorepo into several tsconfigs that depend on each other’s .d.ts. composite: true and references in tsconfig. Builds can be incremental.\n\nLarge repos use this.\n\nIn the code: the comment shows references to ../core. import { add } from \"core\" then add(1, 2). Types come from the referenced project.\n\nEach package needs composite and a proper outDir for this to work.", code: "// tsconfig idea (types only):\n// { \"references\": [{ \"path\": \"../core\" }] }\nimport { add } from \"core\"; // bring in a name\nadd(1, 2); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 66, level: "intermediate", q: "paths aliases?", a: "compilerOptions.paths is only for TypeScript. The bundler must understand the same aliases or runtime imports break.\n\n“It type-checks” must still run.\n\nIn the code: import { Button } from \"@app/ui\" needs the alias on the bundler too. Page returns Button. The path is not a real npm name.\n\nMirror paths in Vite or webpack. Do not only set them in tsconfig.", code: "import { Button } from \"@app/ui\"; // alias must match bundler\nfunction Page() { // a reusable step\n  return Button; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 67, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a namespace?", a: "namespace groups names: namespace A { export ... }. Prefer ES modules today. It still appears in types and declare global.\n\nOlder libraries use this.\n\nIn the code: MathUtil.export function add. MathUtil.add(1, 2) calls it. The namespace name prefixes the function.\n\nFor new code, use a module file and export function add.", code: "namespace MathUtil {\n  export function add(a: number, b: number) { // share this name\n    return a + b; // send this back\n  }\n}\nMathUtil.add(1, 2); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 68, level: "intermediate", q: "declare global?", a: "declare global { ... } from a module adds globals. Remember export {} so the file is a module. Use rarely.\n\nwindow extras use this.\n\nIn the code: export {} makes a module. Window gets PREP_DATA: unknown. That extra is on window. The interface merges with the DOM Window.\n\nWithout export {}, the file might be a script and pollute differently.", code: "export {}; // share this name\ndeclare global {\n  interface Window { // describe the shape\n    PREP_DATA: unknown;     // extra on window\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 69, level: "advanced", q: "this parameter in functions?", a: "function fn(this: HTMLElement, ev: Event) types this without being a real argument. Useful for callbacks that are methods. Callers must bind the right this.\n\nDOM handlers use this.\n\nIn the code: onClick’s this is HTMLButtonElement. this.disabled = true. btn is querySelector(\"button\") with !. addEventListener(\"click\", onClick) passes the button as this.\n\nThe ! asserts btn is not null. Check null in real apps.", code: "function onClick(this: HTMLButtonElement) { // when the user acts\n  this.disabled = true;     // this is the button\n}\nconst btn = document.querySelector(\"button\")!; // a named box\nbtn.addEventListener(\"click\", onClick); // when the user acts" },
    { id: 70, level: "intermediate", q: "function overloads?", a: "Overloads list several ways to call, then one implementation. The implementation must accept the union of all cases. Keep overloads few and specific-first.\n\nUse them when return type depends on the argument.\n\nIn the code: parse(string): number and parse(number): string. The body takes string | number and converts. Specific signatures come first.\n\nIf the implementation cannot accept the union, the overloads are a lie.", code: "function parse(x: string): number; // a reusable step\nfunction parse(x: number): string; // a reusable step\nfunction parse(x: string | number) { // a reusable step\n  return typeof x === \"string\" ? Number(x) : String(x); // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 71, level: "advanced", q: "Why overloads instead of a union param?", a: "Use overloads when the return type depends on a specific argument literal. A single union often loses that link. Generics can also model it.\n\n“If I pass 'json', I get an object” needs that link.\n\nIn the code: make(\"id\") returns number. make(\"name\") returns string. The body returns 1 or \"Ada\". The overloads preserve the link. A single union return would be number | string.\n\nPrefer overloads or generics over a loose union return.", code: "function make(kind: \"id\"): number; // a reusable step\nfunction make(kind: \"name\"): string; // a reusable step\nfunction make(kind: \"id\" | \"name\") { // a reusable step\n  return kind === \"id\" ? 1 : \"Ada\"; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 72, level: "intermediate", q: "class parameter properties?", a: "constructor(private repo: Repo) creates and assigns this.repo in one line. That is a parameter property. Convenient. Some teams avoid it for clarity.\n\nYou will see it in class constructors.\n\nIn the code: Service’s constructor(private name: string) {}. hello() returns this.name. The field was created from the constructor param.\n\nThe field exists even though you never wrote this.name = name.", code: "class Service { // a blueprint\n  constructor(private name: string) {}\n  hello() {\n    return this.name;       // created from the constructor param\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 73, level: "intermediate", q: "public/private/protected in TS?", a: "public is default. private is compile-time only. #field is true JavaScript privacy. protected is for subclasses.\n\nprivate is not a runtime lock unless you use #.\n\nIn the code: password is private, name is public. check() reads this.password.length. The constructor takes both. Outside code cannot read password in the type system.\n\nIf you need real privacy at runtime, use #password.", code: "class User { // a blueprint\n  constructor(private password: string, public name: string) {}\n  check() {\n    return this.password.length > 0; // send this back\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 74, level: "intermediate", q: "abstract class vs interface?", a: "Interfaces have no implementation. Abstract classes can have real methods and cannot be new’ed. Prefer interfaces plus functions unless you need shared code.\n\nDesign talks use this distinction.\n\nIn the code: Named is an interface with name. Animal is abstract with abstract speak and a real hello(). You cannot new Animal. A subclass must write speak.\n\nIf you only need a shape, use an interface.", code: "interface Named { // describe the shape\n  name: string; // shows the idea\n}\nabstract class Animal {\n  abstract speak(): string; // shows the idea\n  hello() { return \"hi\"; }\n}" },
    { id: 75, level: "advanced", q: "implements vs extends?", a: "extends inherits a class (implementation). implements requires matching a shape. A class can implement several interfaces.\n\nOOP questions use these two words.\n\nIn the code: Saveable has save(). File implements Saveable and writes save() that logs ok. There is no extends here.\n\nimplements does not copy methods. You must write them.", code: "interface Saveable { save(): void; } // describe the shape\nclass File implements Saveable { // a blueprint\n  save() { console.log(\"ok\"); }\n}" },
    { id: 76, level: "beginner", q: "How do you make a property readonly?", a: "readonly id: string, or Readonly<T>, locks a field in the type system. Arrays: readonly T[]. Runtime can still mutate unless you freeze.\n\nIds you should not reassign use this.\n\nIn the code: User has readonly id. u.id = \"2\" is commented as a type error. u.name = \"Bob\" is allowed. id is locked. name is not.\n\nreadonly is a type check. Object.freeze is a runtime lock.", code: "type User = { readonly id: string; name: string }; // describe the shape\nconst u: User = { id: \"1\", name: \"Ada\" }; // a named box\nu.name = \"Bob\"; // shows the idea\n// u.id = \"2\";              // type error" },
    { id: 77, level: "intermediate", q: "index signatures?", a: "An index signature [key: string]: number means any string key is allowed, values must match. Known keys must be assignable to that index type.\n\nDictionaries use this.\n\nIn the code: Ages allows any string key to a number. ages.Ada is 21. n is ages[\"Ada\"]. Bob is 22.\n\nIf you also have a named field of a different type, it can conflict with the index signature.", code: "// Example: index signatures?\ntype Ages = { [name: string]: number }; // describe the shape\nconst ages: Ages = { Ada: 21, Bob: 22 }; // a named box\nconst n = ages[\"Ada\"]; // a named box" },
    { id: 78, level: "advanced", q: "What is a homomorphic mapped type?", a: "A homomorphic mapped type over keyof T copies optional and readonly modifiers from T. Partial and Pick are homomorphic. That is why modifiers are preserved.\n\nYour own mapped types should use keyof T when you want that copy.\n\nIn the code: User has name and optional age. Partial<User> keeps both optional-style. p can be {}. Homomorphic mapping copied the optional flag on age.\n\nIf you map over a union of keys that is not keyof T, modifiers may drop.", code: "// Example: What is a homomorphic mapped type?\ntype User = { name: string; age?: number }; // describe the shape\ntype P = Partial<User>;     // name? and age? still optional-style\nconst p: P = {}; // a named box" },
    { id: 79, level: "advanced", q: "keyof any / string index issues?", a: "keyof a string-indexed type is string | number. Object.keys returns string[] even for typed objects. TS cannot prove exact keys at runtime.\n\nLooping keys hits this.\n\nIn the code: keys(u: User) returns Object.keys(u), typed string[]. It is not (\"name\")[]. User only declared name, but runtime can have extra keys.\n\nDo not cast Object.keys to (keyof T)[] unless you own the object and accept the risk.", code: "type User = { name: string }; // describe the shape\nfunction keys(u: User): string[] { // a reusable step\n  return Object.keys(u);    // string[], not (\"name\")[]\n}" },
    { id: 80, level: "intermediate", q: "Why does Object.keys lose types?", a: "Object.keys is not (keyof T)[] because objects can have extra keys at runtime. Typing keys as Array<keyof T> would be a lie. Cast only if you own the object and accept the risk.\n\nLoops over keys need this honesty.\n\nIn the code: u is { name: \"Ada\" }. for (const k of Object.keys(u)) logs k as string. You cannot use u[k] as User[keyof User] without extra work.\n\nIf you need typed keys, keep a const array of keys you listed yourself.", code: "type User = { name: string }; // describe the shape\nconst u: User = { name: \"Ada\" }; // a named box\nfor (const k of Object.keys(u)) { // repeat\n  console.log(k);           // k is string\n}" },
    { id: 81, level: "intermediate", q: "unknown in catch?", a: "useUnknownInCatchVariables makes catch (e) typed unknown. Narrow with e instanceof Error before e.message. Thrown values can be anything.\n\nLook before you assume it is an Error.\n\nIn the code: try JSON.parse(\"{\") throws. catch (e) checks instanceof Error, then logs e.message. That is the safe path.\n\nDo not type catch as any. Use unknown and narrow.", code: "try { // shows the idea\n  JSON.parse(\"{\"); // shows the idea\n} catch (e) {\n  if (e instanceof Error) { // pick a path\n    console.log(e.message); // safe\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 82, level: "beginner", q: "How do you type a Promise?", a: "Promise<User> means a future User. async functions infer Promise of the return type. Annotate public async APIs.\n\nThen await is typed.\n\nIn the code: load returns Promise<{ name: string }> via Promise.resolve. main awaits load(). u.name is string. The return of main is that name.\n\nIf you forget the generic, Promise.resolve({}) may infer too loosely.", code: "function load(): Promise<{ name: string }> { // a future result\n  return Promise.resolve({ name: \"Ada\" }); // a future result\n}\nasync function main() { // can wait inside\n  const u = await load();   // { name: string }\n  return u.name; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 83, level: "intermediate", q: "typing JSON.parse?", a: "JSON.parse returns any by default. A helper that returns T is still an assertion. Better: parse unknown and validate.\n\nAPI payloads need this.\n\nIn the code: parseJson returns unknown from JSON.parse. data is the result of parsing a small JSON string. You must check data before use.\n\nWrap JSON.parse so callers get unknown, not any.", code: "function parseJson(text: string): unknown { // a reusable step\n  return JSON.parse(text);  // treat as unknown\n}\nconst data = parseJson(\"{\\\"n\\\":1}\"); // a named box" },
    { id: 84, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a utility type you would write?", a: "ValueOf<T> = T[keyof T] is a classic small mapped type. Nullable<T> maps each field to T[K] | null. Interviews like a tiny mapped type. You show you understand keyof.\n\nStamp every page “or empty.”\n\nIn the code: ValueOf<User> is string | number. Nullable maps each key. User has name and age. V is the union of field types.\n\nWrite ValueOf when you want every field’s type in one union.", code: "type ValueOf<T> = T[keyof T]; // describe the shape\ntype User = { name: string; age: number }; // describe the shape\ntype V = ValueOf<User>;     // string | number\ntype Nullable<T> = { [K in keyof T]: T[K] | null }; // describe the shape" },
    { id: 85, level: "advanced", q: "DeepPartial?", a: "DeepPartial makes every field optional, and nested objects are DeepPartial too. Handle arrays carefully. Watch infinite recursion on self-referential types.\n\nDeep form patches use this.\n\nIn the code: DeepPartial maps each key, and if T[K] is object, it recurses. p is { addr: {} } for User with addr.city. Nested city can be missing.\n\nStop recursion for Date and arrays if those should not be walked as objects.", code: "type DeepPartial<T> = { // describe the shape\n  [K in keyof T]?: T[K] extends object ? DeepPartial<T[K]> : T[K]; // shows the idea\n};\ntype User = { name: string; addr: { city: string } }; // describe the shape\nconst p: DeepPartial<User> = { addr: {} }; // a named box" },
    { id: 86, level: "intermediate", q: "What is never used for in switches?", a: "In the default branch, assign x to never. If you forget a union member, it will not be never and TS errors. That is an exhaustive check.\n\nWhen you add a new status later, the switch fails to compile until you handle it.\n\nIn the code: hex switches on Color. default const _x: never = c. If a color was forgotten, that line errors. red and blue return hex strings.\n\nAdd a new color to the union, then fix the switch. never will force you.", code: "type Color = \"red\" | \"blue\"; // describe the shape\nfunction hex(c: Color): string { // a reusable step\n  switch (c) {\n    case \"red\": return \"#f00\"; // send this back\n    case \"blue\": return \"#00f\"; // send this back\n    default: {\n      const _x: never = c;  // error if a color was forgotten\n      return _x; // send this back\n    }\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 87, level: "beginner", q: "Can you use TS with Node?", a: "Yes, TypeScript works on the server. Compile to dist, or use a runner in dev. Use @types/node for fs and http types. Set module to match CJS or ESM.\n\nAPIs are a normal use.\n\nIn the code: readFileSync is imported from \"fs\". load(path: string) returns the file text as utf8. Node types make readFileSync known.\n\nInstall @types/node or the checker will not know fs.", code: "import { readFileSync } from \"fs\"; // bring in a name\nfunction load(path: string): string { // a reusable step\n  return readFileSync(path, \"utf8\"); // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 88, level: "intermediate", q: "CJS vs ESM in TS?", a: "module: commonjs emits require. nodenext/ESM often needs .js extensions in import paths (the output paths). Mixing is a common pain; pick one.\n\nImports must run, not only type-check.\n\nIn the code: import { add } from \"./math.js\" is ESM-style. total returns add(a, b). The .js extension is the emit path.\n\nIf you write .ts in the import, Node ESM will not find the file after compile.", code: "import { add } from \"./math.js\"; // ESM-style specifier\nexport function total(a: number, b: number) { // share this name\n  return add(a, b); // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 89, level: "intermediate", q: "What is skipLibCheck?", a: "skipLibCheck skips type-checking .d.ts from node_modules. Your source is still checked. Useful when library types fight each other.\n\nCI gets faster.\n\nIn the code: n: number = 1 is still checked. console.log(n) is fine. The comment says your source is still type-checked. Library .d.ts files are skipped.\n\nDo not use this to hide errors in your own .d.ts. It only skips node_modules by usual setup.", code: "// Example: What is skipLibCheck?\nconst n: number = 1;        // your file still checked\nconsole.log(n); // print to see it\n// your source is still type-checked" },
    { id: 90, level: "advanced", q: "declaration maps?", a: "Declaration maps (.d.ts.map) let Go to Definition in a published package jump to TypeScript source. Good for library authors.\n\nConsumers then land in real .ts, not only .d.ts.\n\nIn the code: export function add is what consumers jump to via maps. a + b is the body. The example is the published function.\n\nEnable declaration and declarationMap in the library tsconfig.", code: "// Example: declaration maps?\nexport function add(a: number, b: number): number { // share this name\n  return a + b;             // consumers can jump here via maps\n}" },
    { id: 91, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you hide a type from the public API?", a: "Do not export the helper type. Infer from functions. Export only what consumers need.\n\nLibrary surface stays small.\n\nIn the code: Hidden is not exported. make() returns { n: number }. x is Hidden inside. Callers see the return shape, not the name Hidden.\n\nIf you export Hidden, you can never rename it without a breaking change.", code: "type Hidden = { n: number }; // not exported\nexport function make(): { n: number } { // share this name\n  const x: Hidden = { n: 1 }; // a named box\n  return x; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 92, level: "advanced", q: "What is a unique symbol?", a: "A unique symbol type is different from every other symbol type. Used in branding. Declare as const.\n\nTwo brands cannot mix.\n\nIn the code: brand is unique symbol. UserId is string & { [brand]: true }. asId casts s as UserId. The symbol is the brand key.\n\nEach unique symbol is its own type. Reusing a normal Symbol() is not the same.", code: "const brand: unique symbol = Symbol(\"user\"); // a named box\ntype UserId = string & { [brand]: true }; // describe the shape\nfunction asId(s: string): UserId { // a reusable step\n  return s as UserId; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 93, level: "intermediate", q: "mixing JS and TS?", a: "Mix JS and TS with allowJs + checkJs, or rename file by file. JSDoc @type can type JS files without renaming.\n\nBrownfield apps migrate slowly.\n\nIn the code: the JSDoc says (a: number, b: number) => number. add(a, b) returns a + b in a .js file. checkJs then type-checks it.\n\nAdd JSDoc or convert to .ts. Do not leave public functions untyped.", code: "/** @type {(a: number, b: number) => number} */\nfunction add(a, b) { // a reusable step\n  return a + b;             // JSDoc types in a .js file\n}" },
    { id: 94, level: "beginner", q: "What is tsc --noEmit?", a: "tsc --noEmit type-checks only. It does not write JS files. Vite or webpack still emit JS. The build fails on type errors.\n\nChecking is separate from bundling.\n\nIn the code: add is what tsc --noEmit flags mistakes on. add(1, 2) is valid. No JS is written by --noEmit. The bundler still emits.\n\nPut tsc --noEmit in CI. Let the bundler emit.", code: "function add(a: number, b: number) { // a reusable step\n  return a + b;             // tsc --noEmit flags mistakes here\n}\nadd(1, 2); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 95, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you type an Express Request?", a: "Extend the Express namespace, or use generics if the types support it. Avoid any on req.body. Validate, then assign a typed variable.\n\nAPIs need a real check, not only a type.\n\nIn the code: Body is { email: string }. handle takes unknown, asserts as Body after a comment about a real validator, then returns data.email. Production should validate first.\n\nNever trust req.body as User without parsing.", code: "type Body = { email: string }; // describe the shape\nfunction handle(body: unknown) { // a reusable step\n  const data = body as Body; // after a real validator in production\n  return data.email; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 96, level: "advanced", q: "Why can generics infer as unknown?", a: "Generics infer as unknown when there is not enough information from arguments. Add a constraint, a default, or an explicit type argument. Expected return type can also help.\n\nA helper then stops being unknown.\n\nIn the code: wrap<T>(x: T) returns T[]. wrap(1) is number[]. wrap<string>(\"a\") is explicit. T comes from x.\n\nIf there is no argument to infer from, pass wrap<User>(...).", code: "function wrap<T>(x: T): T[] { // a reusable step\n  return [x]; // send this back\n}\nconst a = wrap(1);          // number[]\nconst b = wrap<string>(\"a\"); // explicit if needed" },
    { id: 97, level: "intermediate", q: "What is contextual typing?", a: "Contextual typing means callbacks get types from the surrounding function. array.map(x => ...) knows x from the array. addEventListener does the same for the event.\n\nYou did not annotate x, but TS still knows. Less noisy code.\n\nIn the code: n in map is number. doubled is n * 2. forEach n has toFixed. nums is [1, 2, 3]. The array typed the callback.\n\nIf inference fails, annotate the callback parameter.", code: "const nums = [1, 2, 3]; // a named box\nconst doubled = nums.map((n) => n * 2); // n is number\nnums.forEach((n) => { // shows the idea\n  console.log(n.toFixed(0)); // print to see it\n}); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 98, level: "advanced", q: "How would you type a pipe/compose helper?", a: "Typing pipe/compose is a hard puzzle. Use overloads for 2–N functions first. A recursive type over a tuple of functions is the fancy version.\n\nMention overloads before the recursive type. TypeScript has limits here.\n\nIn the code: two overloads for one function and two functions. The implementation takes unknown and Function[] and reduce. pipe(a, f) and pipe(a, f, g) are typed. More arities need more overloads.\n\nShip a few overloads. A perfect infinite pipe type is optional.", code: "function pipe<A, B>(a: A, f: (x: A) => B): B; // a reusable step\nfunction pipe<A, B, C>(a: A, f: (x: A) => B, g: (x: B) => C): C; // a reusable step\nfunction pipe(a: unknown, ...fns: Function[]) { // a reusable step\n  return fns.reduce((v, f) => f(v), a); // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 99, level: "intermediate", q: "readonly arrays and push?", a: "readonly T[] rejects push and splice in the type system. Useful for public function inputs you do not want mutated. Runtime can still cheat if someone casts.\n\nAPIs use this so callers do not mutate your array.\n\nIn the code: sum(nums: readonly number[]) cannot push. reduce computes the total. The push line is commented as a type error.\n\nTake readonly arrays in public functions. Return new arrays if you need to change data.", code: "function sum(nums: readonly number[]) { // a reusable step\n  // nums.push(1);          // type error\n  return nums.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0); // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 100, level: "advanced", q: "How do you keep TS from becoming any-soup?", a: "Keep TypeScript from becoming any-soup: enable strict, ban any in the linter, validate I/O. Prefer unknown. Model states as unions.\n\nType public boundaries first. Tighten locals later. Perfect types on every variable are not the goal.\n\nIn the code: parse takes unknown. It checks object and name, then returns { name }. Otherwise it throws. That is a typed border with no any.\n\nLock the front door (I/O) first. Inner variables can infer.", code: "function parse(data: unknown): { name: string } { // a reusable step\n  if (data && typeof data === \"object\" && \"name\" in data) { // pick a path\n    const name = (data as { name: unknown }).name; // a named box\n    if (typeof name === \"string\") return { name }; // send this back\n  }\n  throw new Error(\"bad\"); // shows the idea\n}" }
  ]
};
